\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The maximal prolongation has square zero}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Let #{k} be a commutative unital ring, let #{X} be a finite set, and
let #{\Omega^1} be a free calculus in the sense of \ref{fdip-00NU},
with derivation #{\covwedge:A\to\Omega^1}. Form the tensor algebra of the
bimodule #{\Omega^1} over #{A}. The \newvocab{maximal prolongation}
is the largest graded algebra
##{A\oplus\Omega^1\oplus\Omega^2\oplus\cdots}
generated by #{A} and #{\Omega^1}, with the given actions of #{A},
which carries an extension of #{\covwedge} to a graded derivation of degree
#{1}. In that extension
##{\covwedge(f\,\covwedge g)=\covwedge f\,\covwedge g}
for all #{f,g\in A}. Products of #{1}-forms obey only the relations
forced by the vanishing already present in #{\Omega^1} and by the
graded Leibniz rule. The identity #{\covwedge^2=0} follows from that rule
on every degree, and it is not imposed separately.}

\proof{
\p{The displayed formula gives
#{\covwedge(\covwedge f)=\covwedge(1\cdot \covwedge f)=\covwedge(1)\,\covwedge f=0}, since #{\covwedge(1)=0}. Thus #{\covwedge^2}
vanishes on functions. Suppose #{\covwedge^2} vanishes on two forms
#{\alpha} and #{\beta}, of degrees #{r} and #{s}. Graded Leibniz
gives
##{\begin{aligned}
\covwedge^2(\alpha\beta)
&=\covwedge\bigl(\covwedge\alpha\cdot\beta+(-1)^r\alpha\cdot \covwedge\beta\bigr)\\
&=\covwedge^2\alpha\cdot\beta+(-1)^{r+1}\covwedge\alpha\cdot \covwedge\beta\\
&\quad+(-1)^r \covwedge\alpha\cdot \covwedge\beta+\alpha\cdot \covwedge^2\beta.
\end{aligned}}
The two middle signs are opposite. The last coefficient is #{+1},
because #{(-1)^r\cdot(-1)^r=1}. The outer terms vanish by the
inductive hypothesis. Every form in the prolongation is a sum of
products of functions and elements #{\covwedge f}, so #{\covwedge^2=0} on the whole
algebra.}
}

\p{The same square-zero extension is the reduction of the universal
envelope by a digraph in \citef{dimakis1994graphs}.
The two calculations that follow specialize it.}
